2. Make the Home credits manually scrollable

The Home credits currently auto-scroll continuously inside the angled glass strip using HomeScreen.update(dt).

Change this behavior so it supports both automatic and manual scrolling.

Required behavior

Automatic behavior

On entering Home, preserve the current slow continuous upward credits movement.

Keep approximately the existing speed (ROLL_SPEED = 22) unless implementation details require a negligible adjustment.

The sequence must continue to loop seamlessly forever.

There must be no visible jump when wrapping between the first and duplicate credits sequence.

Manual interaction

Players must be able to manually move through the Home credits.

Support the practical input methods available to the browser:

* mouse wheel
* trackpad scrolling
* touch dragging/swiping
* pointer dragging where appropriate
* keyboard scrolling when the credits region itself is intentionally focused, if this can be integrated without breaking the existing Home menu navigation

The interaction should feel like scrolling the credits, not like dragging an unrelated decorative element.

Do not make the entire Home screen scroll. Only the credits region inside the glass strip is interactive.

The Play button and existing Home navigation must remain fully functional.

Pause while interacting

As soon as the player begins interacting with the credits:

* stop automatic progression immediately
* allow the user’s movement to control the current credits position
* reset the inactivity timer every time the user continues scrolling/dragging

Use a clear constant such as:

CREDITS_RESUME_DELAY = 2000

or an equivalent 2-second value.

Automatic resume

After approximately 2 seconds with no further credits interaction, automatic upward scrolling must resume.

It must resume:

* from the exact current user-selected position
* at the normal automatic speed
* without snapping back to the old position
* without restarting the credits from the beginning
* without an animation jump
* without briefly moving in the wrong direction

If the player interacts again, pause automatic movement again and restart the inactivity countdown.

Seamless looping during manual movement

Manual scrolling can potentially move the logical offset across a sequence boundary.

Normalize/wrap the credits position so users can continue moving through the repeated sequence naturally.

Do not let manual interaction permanently reach a dead end simply because the content uses two copies for the automatic loop.

The duplicate sequence that exists only to make the visual loop seamless must remain hidden from assistive technology.

⸻

Implementation approach

Choose the cleanest implementation for the existing codebase rather than bolting on a second scrolling system.

A good solution may either:

1. keep rollOffset as the authoritative logical credits position and adjust it directly for wheel/touch/pointer input, or
2. convert the credits viewport to a native scrolling element and make its scrollTop the authoritative position.

Whichever approach you choose, there must be exactly one authoritative position. Do not let a CSS transform and native scrollTop independently accumulate competing offsets.

The current app already has the correct animation architecture:

App.loop() → current screen update(dt)

Use that loop for automatic credits movement.

Prefer tracking something such as:

* current logical offset
* whether the user is actively interacting
* timestamp of the most recent manual credits interaction

Then update(dt) can advance automatically only when:

* Home is active
* reduced motion is not enabled
* the interaction inactivity window has elapsed

Avoid repeating setInterval loops.

If a timeout is used solely to transition interaction state, make sure it cannot stack on repeated Home visits or repeated scrolling.

Event listeners created by HomeScreen must not multiply when leaving/re-entering Home.

⸻

Touch/pointer behavior

The credits strip must work naturally on phones/tablets in landscape.

Ensure the relevant portion of the credits layer can receive pointer input despite the current .home-scene { pointer-events: none; } rule.

Only enable pointer interaction on the credits viewport/column, not the decorative glass generally.

Touch interaction must not trigger the Home Play button or interfere with another region of the screen.

If manually implementing dragging:

* capture the active pointer appropriately
* track vertical deltas
* release cleanly on pointerup / pointercancel
* do not leave the credits permanently in an “interacting” state after cancellation
* use vertical movement in the natural scrolling direction

If native scrolling is used, configure touch-action, overscroll behavior, and overflow so it behaves correctly inside the clipped strip without allowing document scrolling.

⸻

Mouse wheel / trackpad behavior

Wheel/trackpad input over the credits region should:

* manually move the credits by the wheel delta
* pause automatic scrolling
* update the inactivity timestamp
* remain constrained to the credits experience rather than accidentally scrolling the page

Remember that the document itself intentionally does not scroll.

⸻

Keyboard behavior

If the credits region is made keyboard-focusable, make it semantically sensible and visibly focusable without damaging the Home design.

Arrow/Page scrolling while that region is focused may control the credits.

However:

* do not add the credits to the ordinary data-nav Play/Help menu navigation unless doing so is clearly intentional
* do not break the existing spatial menu navigator
* Play should remain the default Home focus
* normal Home arrow navigation should continue to work when the menu is focused

A simple focusable scroll region with an accessible label is preferable to turning the credits into another Home action.

